    model: 'Opioids cause a large, rapid release of dopamine in the brain’s reward pathway, especially the nucleus accumbens, teaching the brain to seek them again. With repeated use the brain adapts by reducing dopamine signaling, so he develops tolerance and needs more to feel the same effect, while ordinary rewards feel flat. Drug cues become conditioned triggers for intense craving, and stopping produces withdrawal, so using to feel normal is powerfully negatively reinforced. Because the teenage prefrontal cortex is still developing, judgment and impulse control are weaker, making compulsive use despite harm more likely. An overdose looks like unresponsiveness, slow or stopped breathing, pinpoint pupils and blue lips; responders should call 911, give naloxone, support breathing, place him in the recovery position, stay with him and give a second dose if he does not respond. Long-term recovery is best supported by medication for opioid use disorder, such as buprenorphine, methadone or extended-release naltrexone, combined with behavioral treatment such as cognitive behavioral therapy, family therapy or contingency management, plus recovery supports and a relapse prevention plan.',
