    model: 'For centuries, people with mental illness were confined in harsh asylums such as Bethlem. In the 1790s Philippe Pinel introduced moral treatment, and in the 1840s Dorothea Dix persuaded states to build mental hospitals in the United States, which later became overcrowded. Freud’s psychoanalysis shaped therapy in the early 1900s, and in the 1950s chlorpromazine, the first antipsychotic, made it possible for many people to live outside hospitals. Medications, civil rights concerns about inhumane conditions and the high cost of institutions drove deinstitutionalization, supported by the 1963 Community Mental Health Act. However, community mental health centers were never fully funded, so many people lost care, contributing to homelessness and the large numbers of people with mental illness in jails and prisons. Modern efforts to close these gaps include the 2008 Mental Health Parity and Addiction Equity Act requiring equal insurance coverage, the Olmstead decision under the ADA guaranteeing community-based services, Certified Community Behavioral Health Clinics and assertive community treatment for comprehensive community care, collaborative care in primary care, supported housing, and the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline launched in 2022 to strengthen crisis response.',
